  "title": "Creation is decided at the gluing line",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Creation is decided at the gluing line, in two rounds. For every near side (any complement-closed set of assignments showing the gluing line's six patterns, in particular every space minus a removal) and each of the four census far sides, the crossing knots of up to three points that the elimination closure reaches are exactly these: in round one, a knot {c, x, y} (c far, x near off the line, y on the line) for which some z on the line makes {c, y, z} a far knot and {x, y, z} a near knot; in round two, a knot {c, x, w} for which x and w share a round-one point y, and a knot {c, c', x} for which some y on the line gives two of {c, c', y} (a far knot), {c, x, y} and {c', x, y} (round one). So a crossing knot is new exactly when its near and far parts fail to meet this way through the gluing line, and elimination through the line's points alone reaches everything. Proved: the rule's steps are closure steps, and every closure step stays inside the rule on every local configuration (every near relation on the line and up to three further classes against every far class, pair and triple), checked exhaustively.",
